## 2026-08-19 — Task: Fix the standard-deduction spillover bug in totalTaxWithLTCG

Bug-fix iteration. I started on the topmost backlog item (Roth conversion
sizing), and while reading `totalTaxWithLTCG` to plan the binary search I found
a real arithmetic error from the LTCG-stacking iteration. Per the loop rules,
fixing it became this iteration's task; the Roth item is untouched and is still
topmost for the next run.

### The bug

    const ordinaryTaxable = Math.max(0, ordinaryIncome + taxableSS - standardDeduction);
    const totalTaxable = ordinaryTaxable + ltcg;   // <-- wrong

`ordinaryTaxable` is clamped at 0, and then the FULL `ltcg` was added on top.
So whenever ordinary income + taxable SS came in under the standard deduction,
the unused part of the deduction simply evaporated instead of offsetting the
gains stacked above it. The LTCG band handed to the 0/15/20% bracket walk was
too wide by exactly the unabsorbed deduction.

Measured on the pre-fix code (probe test, deleted after use):
- single, $0 ordinary / $0 SS / $100,000 LTCG → charged $7,747.50; correct
  $5,385.00. Overstated by $2,362.50 = 15% x the whole $15,750 deduction.
- mfj, same inputs → charged $495; correct $0 (taxable income 68,500 sits
  entirely inside the $96,700 0% bracket).
- the LTCG marginal-rate chart started charging tax at $48,350 of gains instead
  of $64,100 ( = 15,750 deduction + 48,350 zero-bracket ). The visible 0% zone
  was short by a full standard deduction.

### Why the old line looked right

It IS right in the common case. As soon as ordinary income + taxable SS exceeds
the deduction, `ordinaryTaxable > 0` and `ordinaryTaxable + ltcg` equals
`ordinary + ss + ltcg - deduction`, so the two expressions agree. The app's own
defaults (ordinary $30k, SS $23,712) sit in that region, which is why neither
the existing tests nor the chart ever looked wrong. It only breaks in the
low-ordinary-income / large-gains corner — which is precisely the "realize gains
in a low-income year" case this app exists to advise on.

### The fix

    const totalTaxable = Math.max(0, ordinaryIncome + taxableSS + ltcg - standardDeduction);

The LTCG band stays `[ordinaryTaxable, totalTaxable]`, which now narrows
automatically when the deduction spills past ordinary income. Authority: Form
1040 subtracts the deduction from AGI exactly once, and the Qualified Dividends
and Capital Gain Tax Worksheet caps the preferentially-taxed amount at line 1
(total taxable income) — it never lets the gain exceed taxable income. Hand-check
of the single $100k case against that worksheet: line 1 = 84,250, line 7 =
max(0, 84,250 - 100,000) = 0, gain taxed = 84,250, of which 48,350 @ 0% and
35,900 @ 15% = $5,385. Matches.

### Tests

+3 in `src/utils/tax.test.ts`, 62 -> 65 passing:
- `lets the unused standard deduction offset LTCG` — pins $5,385 single and $0
  MFJ, with the pre-fix wrong numbers written into the comment so a future
  regression is recognisable on sight.
- `starts taxing LTCG only after the deduction and the 0% bracket are used up` —
  asserts the first non-zero marginal rate lands at $64,100 single and $128,200
  MFJ.
- `never taxes more than total taxable income across the LTCG sweep` — a
  property check over an ordinary x SS x LTCG grid, cross-checking against a
  direct AGI-minus-deduction computation (tax <= 37% of taxable income, and
  exactly 0 when taxable income is 0). This is the invariant the old code
  violated, so it guards the class of bug rather than the one instance.

Also corrected the stale comment on the pre-existing
`taxes LTCG at 0% when total taxable income stays below the threshold` test,
which asserted the right answer for the wrong reason (it claimed
totalTaxable = 10,000; it is now max(0, 10,000 - 15,750) = 0). No existing
assertion needed changing — all four LTCG tests that reason about bracket
positions use ordinary income above the deduction, so they were unaffected.

### Sanity check that the fix did not flatten the feature

At app defaults (SS $23,712, ordinary $30,000) the LTCG curve segments to
`10.2% [0-10500] hill | 0% [10750-13750] valley | 15% [14000+] flat`. The 10.2%
is 12% x 0.85 — the torpedo dragging benefits in — and it ends at $10,562 of
gains where the 85% cap binds, which I verified by hand from the provisional
income. The >25% stacking hump asserted by the existing
`shows elevated marginal rates from SS torpedo stacking` test (SS $30k,
ordinary $30k) still passes. So the stacking story is intact; only the
deduction accounting moved.

Verified: `npx tsc -b`, `npm run lint`, `npm test` (65 passed), `npm run build`
all clean — pre-existing >500kB chunk warning only.

Note for the next run: the Roth conversion sizing item is still the topmost
unchecked backlog bullet and is now safe to build on, since `maxConversionUnder`
was going to binary-search over this exact chain and would have inherited the
error.

