Iteration: Age-65+ additional standard deduction
================================================================================

Task: add an "age 65 or older" toggle (plus a "both spouses 65+" toggle for
MFJ) adding $2,000 (single/HOH) or $1,600 per qualifying spouse (MFJ) on top of
the 2025 base standard deduction.

### The figures

Rev. Proc. 2024-40 section 2.15: the additional standard deduction for the aged
or blind is $1,600 per qualifying person, raised to $2,000 for someone who is
unmarried and not a surviving spouse. So single/HOH $2,000 (max one), MFJ
$1,600 per spouse (max two). Blindness stacks the same amounts again; this app
models age only and the code comment says so.

New exports in tax.ts:
- `ADDITIONAL_STD_DEDUCTION_65: Record<FilingStatus, number>` = {2000, 1600}
- `maxSeniors(fs)` -> 1 for single, 2 for mfj
- `standardDeductionFor(fs, seniors)` -> base + clamp(seniors) * addition

`standardDeductionFor` is now the ONLY place the deduction is read for a
computation. Three call sites moved off `FILING_PARAMS[fs].standardDeduction`:
`totalTax`, `totalTaxWithLTCG`, `conversionMeasureValue`. FILING_PARAMS keeps
the base figure (App.tsx still reads it directly, to show "base plus addition"
in the UI note).

### Plumbing: seniors is a new positional param

Added `seniors = 0` after `filingStatus` on totalTax, marginalRateCurve,
totalTaxWithLTCG, ltcgMarginalRateCurve, conversionMeasureValue,
maxConversionUnder, sizeConversion. Default 0 = every existing caller is
unchanged, which the "defaults to the base deduction everywhere" test pins.

BUG I CAUSED AND CAUGHT MID-ITERATION, worth remembering: maxConversionUnder
and sizeConversion end in `maxConversion = 1_000_000`, so inserting `seniors`
before it silently rebound App.tsx's positional `MAX_CONVERSION` argument to
`seniors` (1,000,000 seniors, clamped to 1 -> wrong deduction). Two App tests
went red and pointed straight at it. Also had to fix one tax.test.ts call site
(`sizeConversion(..., 'single', 1_000)` -> `..., 'single', 0, 1_000`). This is
why I added the backlog bullet about converting these to an options object:
seven positional params is already past the point where insertion is safe, and
the OBBBA/muni/QCD/tax-year items each want to add another.

`conversionCeilings()` needed NO change — every ceiling is measured against a
quantity that is either pre-deduction (provisional income, MAGI) or already
net of it (taxable income), so the amounts do not move.

### Numbers this pinned down

sd single 0/1/2 -> 15,750 / 17,750 / 17,750 (clamped)
sd mfj 0/1/2/3 -> 31,500 / 33,100 / 34,700 / 34,700 (clamped)

- No benefits: the first taxed dollar moves 15,751 -> 17,751, exactly +$2,000.
- Single, $30k other income, avg benefit: tax 2,812.81 -> 2,572.81, i.e.
  2,000 x 12%. MFJ at the same income is still in the 10% bracket, so each
  spouse's 1,600 saves exactly 160.
- THE INTERESTING ONE: the 0%-rate valley widens by LESS than the addition.
  Last 0%-rate sample moves 14,750 -> 16,000, and the exact crossing moves
  14,881 -> 16,214 — about +$1,333, not +$2,000. Once provisional income clears
