## Iteration: Muni-interest toggle (tax-exempt interest)

Task: "Muni-interest toggle: add a tax-exempt interest slider... Show the
taxable-SS delta and the implied tax cost of each muni dollar beside the chart."

### The rule being modelled

IRC 103 excludes municipal bond interest from gross income, so it never reaches
Form 1040 line 15 and is never itself taxed. But IRC 86(b)(2)(B) adds it
straight back on Pub 915 Worksheet 1 line 4, so provisional income treats it
exactly like a pension check. The only thing it can move is the taxable share of
benefits — which is why the cost is invisible: the tax lands on line 6b, next to
benefits the retiree cannot control, nowhere near the bonds that caused it.

Three different income definitions had to stay straight, and they are NOT the
same MAGI:
- provisional income (SS taxability): muni IS added.
- IRMAA MAGI: muni IS added (AGI + tax-exempt interest).
- senior deduction MAGI (IRC 151(d)(5)): muni is NOT added back — it is AGI plus
  the 911/931/933 exclusions only. So muni can never touch the OBBBA phaseout
  except indirectly, through the benefits it drags into AGI.
AGI itself, of course, never contains muni.

### The positional-argument trap, dodged rather than fixed

The previous iteration's note warned that inserting `seniors` positionally had
silently reassigned App.tsx's `MAX_CONVERSION`. This task needed a new parameter
on eight functions, so the same trap was waiting. Dodge: `muniInterest` was
appended as the LAST parameter everywhere, including *after* `maxConversion` on
`maxConversionUnder`/`sizeConversion`, where it reads badly but cannot reassign
an existing argument. All 103 pre-existing tests passed unchanged on the first
run, which is the evidence the dodge worked. The options-object refactor is
still backlogged and is now more urgent, not less — `sizeConversion` is at eight
positional args (bullet updated to say eight).

`sizeConversion` also picked up a local `taxAt(ordinary)` closure, because the
same six arguments were being repeated four times; `taxAfterRaw` went away with
it (it was only ever rounded once).

### New API

`muniInterestEffect(muniInterest, ordinaryIncome, ssBenefit, ltcg, filingStatus,
seniors)` -> taxableSSWithout/With/Delta, taxWithout/With, taxCost,
costPerDollar (average, %), ratePerNextDollar (marginal, %). Rounding follows
`sizeConversion`: costPerDollar is derived from the ROUNDED taxCost so the
displayed cents and the displayed dollars agree.

### Numbers worth keeping (single, average benefit $23,712)

- $20,000 other income, $5,000 muni: provisional 31,856 -> 36,856, so the muni
  straddles the $34,000 threshold. Taxable benefits 3,428 -> 6,927.60
  (2,144 at 50% + 2,856 at 85%). All inside the 10% bracket: $350 of tax on
  "tax-free" interest = 7.0c per dollar, next dollar 8.5c.
- Same $5,000 as ordinary income instead: $1,702.81 vs $1,117.76 — the gap is
  the interest being taxed itself, which the muni escapes.
- $30,000 other income (the app default), $5,000 muni: fully in the 85% band, so
  delta is exactly 0.85 x 5,000 = $4,250, tax $510, and costPerDollar ==
  ratePerNextDollar == 10.2% (= 0.85 x 12%). Nice coincidence for a UI test.
- $100,000 other income: 85% cap already binds, muni is genuinely free (tax
  identical to the dollar, with or without seniors).
- Curve shift: first taxed dollar moves 15,000 -> 11,750 with $10,000 of muni.
  NOT a 1:1 shift — muni raises taxable income only through the benefits it
  drags in, at 50c on the dollar in that band, and the standard deduction still
  has to be cleared.
- $20,000 ordinary + $20,000 LTCG: provisional is already 51,856, leaving only
  $477.60 under the 85% cap, so $5,000 of muni drags in $478 and the next dollar
  is free. My first draft of that test asserted the delta would be *larger* with
  gains; it is smaller, because the gains spent the torpedo first. Corrected.
- Conversion sizing at the default scenario is unchanged at $14,069 with or
  without muni (the 85% cap binds at that conversion either way), but taxBefore
  moves 2,813 -> 3,323 and costPerDollar 19.65% -> 16.03%. The conversion looks
  cheaper only because the muni was charged first.

