## State treatment of Social Security benefits (`src/utils/stateTax.ts`)

### What it does

A new `STATE_SS_RULES` table plus a "Does your state tax it too?" section
below the IRMAA one. Nine states for 2025, eight for 2026. Each row carries a
mechanism, a per-year income test, the rule in prose, and a citation;
`statesTaxingSocialSecurity(year)` filters on `exemptFrom`, which only West
Virginia has.

Deliberately not computed, and the section says so twice. No two of these
rules share a shape — Colorado runs off age bands, Connecticut caps the taxed
share at 25% of benefits *received*, Minnesota has two competing methods,
Montana has no rule at all, New Mexico and Rhode Island are cliffs, Utah works
through a credit against the flat rate, Vermont prorates over $10,000, West
Virginia was mid-phase-out. Nine wrong models are worse than nine accurate
pointers.

### Every figure verified against a primary source

- CO — HB24-1142, 55–64 band at $75k/$95k from tax year 2025; 65+ unlimited.
- CT — $75k single/separate, $100k joint/HOH; 25% cap. OLR 2025-R-0152.
- MN — 2025 $108,320/$84,490/$54,160; 2026 $110,780/$86,410/$55,390, off the
  Department of Revenue's Tax Year 2026 Inflation-Adjusted Amounts.
- MT — § 15-30-2120 confirmed as "Adjustments to federal taxable income", and
  § 15-30-2110 confirmed repealed by Ch. 503, L. 2021 (SB 399), so the former
  Montana-only benefit worksheet really is gone as of 2024.
- NM — § 7-2-5.14 "Exemption — social security income…"; $100k/$150k/$75k;
  2026 HB 92 (which would have phased in an exemption above the caps) was
  postponed indefinitely on 22 Jan 2026, so the cliff stands.
- RI — see below.
- UT — SB 71 (2025) raised the credit thresholds from $45k/$75k/$37k to
  $54k/$90k/$45k, retroactive to 2025; 2.5¢-per-dollar phase-out.
- VT — S.51 / Act 71 (2025), retroactive to 1 Jan 2025: $55k/$70k full,
  prorated to zero at $65k/$80k. Not indexed.
- WV — 35% / 65% / 100% exempt for 2024 / 2025 / 2026, so the count of taxing
  states drops nine → eight exactly when the year selector flips.

The eight-state 2026 list was cross-checked independently and matches the
table with WV removed — nothing was added for 2026 either.

### Two things the sources disagreed about

**Rhode Island's joint threshold.** The 2025 Taxable Social Security Income
Worksheet says $133,750. PUB 2026-01 (the Retirement Income Tax Guide,
published Feb 2026) says $133,500 — eight times, in the threshold list and in
every worked example. Neither is a slip you can dismiss by repetition.

Resolved by arithmetic, and it is worth remembering the trick: § 44-30-12(c)(8)
indexes the two base amounts, $80,000 and $100,000, by one shared COLA factor.
So the joint limit is always exactly 1.25× the single one. $107,000 × 1.25 =
$133,750, and 2024's published pair ($104,200 / $130,250) holds to the same
ratio. $133,500 would imply a single threshold of $106,800. The worksheet is
right and the guide has a typo; a comment in `stateTax.ts` records why.

**Whether Rhode Island has published 2026 figures.** It has not, and the
reason is not the one I first wrote down. ADV 2025-22 (3 Nov 2025) is titled
"Tax Year 2026 Tables", but its own contents page reads "The following items
are for the 2025 Tax Year: Social Security and pension modification amounts…"
— RI indexes this one modification a year behind the rest of its tables. The
advisory list has no 2026 entry on inflation adjustments (the most recent is
ADV 2026-17, 31 July 2026), so 2025's limits really are the latest on file and
the row says so instead of reprinting them under a 2026 heading.

### Fetch notes for the next iteration

CMS was the blocked source last time; this time it was tax.ri.gov, law.justia
and mca.legmt, all 403 to WebFetch. `curl -sL` gets the PDFs fine. There is no
`pdftotext` on this machine and no `pypdf`/`pdfminer` in the system Python, so
the extractor that worked was: pull every `stream…endstream`, zlib-inflate
what inflates, then pull `(…)` literals out of the content stream. That reads
Type1/TrueType PDFs (both RI worksheets, the retirement guide). It does *not*
read CID/Type0 PDFs — ADV 2025-22 came back as bookmark text only, which was
still enough to settle the question above. WebFetch refuses these PDFs
outright.

### Bugs found in the in-progress work

The working tree already held an unfinished version of this task from a
crashed iteration. Two defects in it, both fixed here:

- `.state-rules dt:first-child { margin-top: 0 }` matched *every* `dt`, because
  each one is the first child of its own wrapper `<div>`. The 0.875rem gap
  between rules never rendered. Now `.state-rules > div:first-child dt`.
- The Rhode Island 2026 cell explained the gap as "Rhode Island sets each
  year's limits the autumn before", which is backwards — see above.

Also worth knowing: `role="note"` is spoken for. Adding it to the new warning
paragraph broke seven existing tests that use `getByRole('note')` to find the
MFS warning specifically. Left as a bare `.warning-note`.

