## Split the page into six tabs

`ralph/PLAN.md`: "Page has gotten long. Split into tabs. For example, tab 1
should be just about the SS 50-85 tax torpedo."

### The split, and why it is by subject rather than by input

Eleven sections had accumulated on one scroll. The grouping:

| Tab | Sections |
| --- | --- |
| Tax Torpedo | main marginal-rate chart, tax-exempt-interest pricing, the three `<details>` explainers |
| Capital Gains | LTCG stacking |
| Medicare | IRMAA cliffs |
| Strategies | Roth conversion sizing, QCD, lump-sum election |
| Over Time | multi-year projection, withdrawal sequencing |
| State Taxes | state reference table |

The load-bearing decision was *not* the grouping. It was that the scenario
inputs cannot live in a panel. Six sections price against `ordinaryIncome` and
five against `plannedLtcg`, so co-locating either slider with its consumers is
arithmetically impossible — there is no tab that contains all of them. Both
sliders moved out of the sections that happened to own them (ordinary income
was inside Capital Gains Stacking, planned LTCG inside Roth conversion sizing)
and up into a shared block above the tab strip, joining year, filing status,
age, benefit, muni and QCD. Eight inputs, one scenario, six angles on it.

That is the same fix three separate "Discovered work" bullets had been asking
for — the muni, IRMAA and QCD sections each said "the sliders further down",
pointing two and three sections past themselves. All three sentences now say
"above" and mean it. Flipped those three to [x].

### What the move breaks that a test cannot catch

A slider named "Other Ordinary Income" now sits directly above a chart whose
x-axis *is* other ordinary income, and the same for LTCG on the Capital Gains
tab. In both cases the slider is a *point* the prose prices against, not the
curve's input — the curve sweeps that axis 0→max regardless. Nothing on screen
distinguishes the two roles. Logged as backlog: a labelled ReferenceLine at the
slider's value on both charts would.

### Panels unmount rather than hide

`{tab === id && <div role="tabpanel">…}`. Hiding with the `hidden` attribute
would have kept `getByText` working in the suite, but `getByRole` — 83 of the
call sites — filters the accessibility tree either way, so `hidden` would have
bought nothing and left six Recharts trees rendering on every keystroke.

82 tests failed on the unmount, exactly the ones asserting inside a panel. They
now render through a `renderTab(name)` helper that clicks the tab first. Whole
describes moved for the single-subject blocks (state treatment, projection,
sequencing, lump sum); the mixed ones (`App`, `tax year selector`,
`head of household`, `qualified charitable distribution`) moved test by test,
which is itself informative — the six QCD tests left on plain `render(<App />)`
are exactly the ones that only touch the shared slider.

Two more failures were prose assertions pinning sentences the move invalidated
("No cliff falls inside the chart above" — there is no chart on the Medicare
tab; "Nothing here feeds the charts above or the projections below" — they are
a tab away now). Ten such cross-references were rewritten in `App.tsx`, four of
them naming the destination tab outright.

### Roving tabindex

Arrow keys move selection, `tabIndex` is 0 on the selected tab and -1 on the
rest, and focus follows. Selection-follows-focus is the right variant here:
every panel is cheap to mount, so there is nothing to defer with the
manual-activation dance the APG offers for expensive panels. Seven new tests
cover the strip itself, including the one that matters most — that the shared
sliders keep their values across all six tabs.

### Verification

npx tsc -b, npm run lint, npx vitest run (424 passed, up from 417),
npm run build — all clean; only the pre-existing >500kB chunk warning.

