## Both years in the state table
## 2026-08-20

The state-treatment table printed one year's income test at a time, which hid
the contrast the year selector exists to create. Nine rules, and switching the
selector changed exactly two cells — but you could only find that out by
flipping back and forth and holding the old wording in your head.

### A delta line, not a second column

The backlog note offered both. A second column would have been blank for six of
the nine rows, and both columns carry sentences rather than figures, so the
table would have doubled in width to say nothing new most of the time.

Instead each income-test cell now prints the selected year's wording and, only
where some other selectable year reads differently, that year's underneath —
set back behind a rule and dimmed, because the selected year is the answer and
the other is the comparison. The `tbody td` count stays at 18 rather than 27,
which is a fact one existing test already asserted and now asserts on purpose.

### The count is the point, not a footnote

`statesWithMovingTests(year)` drives a new lead sentence: *"Most of these are as
frozen as the federal $25,000: 6 of the 9 rules below read word for word the
same in 2025 as in 2026."* That sentence is the whole reason the section exists
on a page about frozen thresholds — the federal $25,000 has not moved since
1983, and neither have six of the nine states that still reach the benefit.

The three that do move for 2025 move for three different reasons, and the delta
line shows each without the prose having to enumerate them:

- **Minnesota** re-indexes annually — $108,320 joint becomes $110,780.
- **Rhode Island** indexes a year behind and has not published the next set, so
  the 2026 cell says so and the 2025 figures survive only as the comparison
  beneath it. That is the one row where the delta carries the only numbers on
  screen.
- **West Virginia** is not a threshold move at all: its phase-out completes, so
  the 2025 row reads "AGI ≤ $50,000 single / $100,000 joint: exempt" over
  "2026 Exempt at every income".

That last one is why the lead sentence says "rules" and not "thresholds" —
West Virginia's threshold does not move, its rule does. First draft said
thresholds and was quietly wrong about a third of its own example set.

### The count changes with the year, and had to

For 2026 the moving set is two, not three: West Virginia has left the table
entirely by then, so it cannot be one of the rows shown twice. `stateTestDeltas`
is computed per rule against `TAX_YEARS`, but `statesWithMovingTests` filters
through `statesTaxingSocialSecurity(year)` first, which is what keeps the prose
count and the rendered rows in agreement. A test asserts exactly that — that
nothing is ever flagged as moving in a year it is not listed in.

### Comparison is on the display string

Not on parsed figures. A state whose statute changed shape but kept its numbers
still reads differently and should be shown twice; a state that reprints the
same sentence has not moved for the reader's purposes regardless of what its
legislature did. The existing `moves the thresholds of exactly the states that
index them` unit test already relied on that equality, so this is the same
notion of "moved" the data layer already had, now surfaced.

### The JSX whitespace trap

`<span>{delta.year}</span>` followed by `{delta.test}` on the next line renders
as `2026Full: AGI < $110,780...`. JSX drops whitespace-only lines between
elements, and the CSS `margin-right` on the year hides it completely from the
eye — it is only wrong to anything reading `textContent`, which includes screen
readers. Fixed with an explicit `{' '}` and pinned by `toMatch(/^2026 Full: /)`
so it cannot come back.

### Two existing assertions had to go, and that was the deliverable

`drops West Virginia and re-prices the indexed states for 2026` asserted
`not.toHaveTextContent('$108,320')` and `not.toHaveTextContent('$133,750')` —
i.e. that the previous year's figures appear nowhere in the section. Making
them appear *is* this change. They are now scoped: the selected year's figure
must be in `.state-test-current` and must **not** be, and the previous year's
must be in `.state-test-delta` and must **not** be in the current cell. That is
strictly stronger than the old blanket `not` — a stale copy-paste in either
direction now fails, where before only one direction did.

### Verification

npx tsc -b, npm run lint, npx vitest run (440 passed, up from 431 — seven new
unit tests on `stateTestDeltas` / `statesWithMovingTests`, two new App tests on
which rows print twice), npm run build — all clean; only the pre-existing
>500kB chunk warning. Rendered output dumped and read end to end, which is how
the "thresholds"/"rules" slip and the missing space were caught.

### Logged as discovered work

The calendar has reached 2026, so the Rhode Island "Not published yet" line was
written about a year that is now in progress — that entry, Minnesota's 2026
figures and West Virginia's completed phase-out all want re-reading against the
source publications, and 2027 is now a plausible third `TAX_YEARS` entry. Worth
noting a third year would put two delta lines under every moving row; the
markup handles it, the density may not.

Also flipped the `.tier-table` note to [x]: its one open question was whether
the state-treatment column should happen on its own merits now the dependency
had evaporated. It should have, and it has.

================================================================================
Anchor the projection on published figures instead of indexing into them
================================================================================

`projectYearParams` indexed forward from the *start* year every time, so a
projection that started in 2025 modelled 2026 by running the inflation slider
over Rev. Proc. 2024-40 — while Rev. Proc. 2025-32 sat in `TAX_YEAR_PARAMS`
unread. At a 0% assumption the section reported a 2026 standard deduction of
$15,750 when the form says $16,100.

### The fix is three lines

`publishedAnchorYear(year)` returns the latest year in `TAX_YEARS` at or below
the target; `projectYearParams` reads that year's figures and indexes only
`year - anchor` years past it. So 2025 -> 2026 is a lookup, not an estimate,
and the slider first bites in 2027. `sequencing.ts` shares the helper and got
the fix without a line changing.

Scanned rather than `TAX_YEARS[last]`-compared, so a gap in the years on file
(2025 and 2027 but not 2026) anchors 2026 on 2025 instead of on a year with no
parameters behind it. Same reasoning `defaultTaxYear` already used.

### The kink is real and is now said out loud

At an assumed 5% the brackets still widen by the actual ~2.7% in the one year
that is already law, and the curve bends at 2027. `Projection.publishedThrough
Year` reports how far the published run reaches — `startYear` when the horizon
opens on the newest year on file, which is the ordinary case, so the UI has
something to test before it promises anything. A field note under the COLA
slider and a clause on the chart caption both hang off it and disappear for a
2026 start.

Computed by extending one year at a time from `startYear` rather than reading
the last published row, so a gap stops the run instead of being jumped over.

### The COLA was anchored too, then backed out

First pass added `publishedColaFactor`, growing the benefit by each year's
published COLA where the SSA has announced one. Same class of correction —
the January 2026 raise of 2.8% is announced, not assumed — and it looked right
until the tests showed what it did to the section's control case: at a 0% COLA
the taxable share went 47.14% -> 47.01%, and the prose branch that says "at a
0% COLA nothing moves: the taxable share sits at 47.14% for all 20 years"
became false.

The asymmetry that decided it: the brackets have no user control except through
the inflation slider, so an assumption there is simply wrong output. The
benefit is the reader's own figure on their own slider, and the filer's *other*
income has no published figure to anchor to — so anchoring one and not the
other stops "hold your income flat in real terms" from being true of the chart.
Anchoring the brackets costs nothing there, because provisional income does not
move: a test pins that the taxable share is still perfectly flat at 0%.

Backed out rather than shipped half-argued, and logged as discovered work with
the argument attached, since deciding it means also deciding what the control
case should then say.

### Re-baselined figures, and why each moved

Eight assertions moved by a few dollars — the 2026 deduction is larger and the
brackets wider, so any horizon crossing 2026 from a 2025 start prices
differently. Lifetime sequencing tax $77,757 -> $77,775, the projection's real
last-year tax $4,277 -> $4,278, and so on. None of them changed direction or
crossed a threshold the prose keys off, which is the check that mattered.

One sequencing test needed rewriting rather than re-baselining: "taxes the same
amount every year when nothing moves" ran 2025-2028 at a 0% COLA and now sees
two distinct bills. It starts at 2026 instead, where no published Rev. Proc.
lands inside the horizon — and a second test asserts the 2025 case explicitly:
the bill falls once at 2026 and is flat from there.

### Verification

npx tsc -b, npm run lint, npx vitest run (449 passed, up from 440 — six new
unit tests on the anchoring, `publishedThroughYear` and the flat-share
property, two new App tests on the field note and the published 2026 read),
npm run build — all clean; only the pre-existing >500kB chunk warning. Rendered
output read end to end to check the new note's wording in place.

Worth knowing: `App.test.tsx` pins the clock to 2025-07-01, so the default test
scenario is exactly the case this change affects. Once 2027 figures land, a
2025 start will read two published years and the note will print a range.

================================================================================
Branch the shortfall sentence: not every shortfall is every order's
================================================================================

`compareSequencing` reported `anyShortfall = strategies.some(...)`, and the one
paragraph hanging off it read as universal: "These accounts do not last 20
years ... every order is spending the same nothing." The open question was
whether some-but-not-all was reachable. It is.

### It is reachable, and it is not rare at the margin

Swept 16,128 scenarios (spending x three balances x growth x horizon): 74 came
back with one or two orders dry and the rest funding the horizon out. The
mechanism is the one the note guessed at — the three draw on identical accounts
but do not drain them at the same rate, because the tax each pays along the way
is cash leaving the household too, and bracket filling pays some of it years
early by design. Every shape shows up: bracket filling alone dry, conventional
alone dry, two dry with proportional surviving, and once the reverse — bracket
filling the only survivor, its early withdrawals having kept the later RMD from
compounding into a bigger bill.

### The sharper finding: failure was winning

In 49 of those 74, the order that ran out of money posted the *lowest* lifetime
tax and collected the table's `seq-best` highlight. A household with nothing
left has nothing left to withdraw, and nothing withdrawn is nothing taxed — so
running dry ends the tax bill exactly as avoiding it does, and the headline
score could not tell the two apart. Reachable in three slider moves from the
app's defaults: taxable $25k, IRA $150k, Roth $0 gives bracket filling $89,677
against conventional's $91,458, while it is $6,536 short in 2044 and finishes
at $0 to conventional's $2,996.

That was not in the note's scope, but it is the same defect: `anyShortfall`
being a `some` meant nothing downstream knew *which* order failed, so nothing
downstream could refuse to reward it.

### What changed

`StrategyProjection.firstShortfallYear` — the year an order stopped being a
plan, not just how many years it was short. Once dry it stays dry (a test pins
that), so the first year is the whole story.

`SequencingComparison` gains `allShortfall`, `shortfallStrategies` and
`lowestTaxSolvent` — the cheapest order that funded every year, null when none
did. `lowestTax` is left alone: it is a factual "cheapest of the three" and the
prose needs both figures to make the contrast.

The prose is now three-armed. All dry keeps the old paragraph. Some dry names
the orders, dates the failure, sums the unfunded spending, and then splits
again: if the dry order holds the lowest tax it says so and points at
`lowestTaxSolvent` as the figure to compare against ("That is not a saving");
if it does not, it says the figure is understated and leaves it there.

The table stopped rewarding the failure two ways: the best-cell on lifetime tax
is now the cheapest *solvent* order, and the best-cell on ending value is
suppressed when every order ends at zero — three winners in a three-way tie at
nothing is not a reading. A `.seq-dry` line under the order's name says "Ran out
in 2044" so the table stands up without the paragraph.

### Wording that had to be year-agnostic

First draft said "every year after 2044 cost it nothing" — but the reachable
default-adjacent case runs dry in the *final* year, so "after" named an empty
set. Both arms now read "from {year} on", which is true whether the shortfall
starts in the last year or the tenth.

