## A recurring gift threaded through both Over Time sections
## 2026-08-21

Found the working tree dirty again, the same way as last iteration: App.tsx,
both Over Time utils and all three test files carried a finished-looking
implementation of the QCD-threading task that never reached a commit. PLAN.md
in the tree had already flipped the bullet and logged a follow-up. Verified it
rather than redoing it — `npx tsc -b`, `npm run lint`, `npx vitest run
src/utils` (324 passed) and `npx vitest run src/App.test.tsx` (175 passed, up
from 168) and `npm run build` are all clean — so this iteration finished and
shipped it.

### What the change does

The QCD slider on the Strategies tab priced a single year and said, in a field
note, that the projection and the sequencing comparison deliberately left it
out. That note is now false in the right direction: both sections read the same
slider as a gift repeated every year.

Three things had to be got right for that to mean anything:

- **It comes out of the balance, not out of other income.** The single-year
  section has no balance, so it reads the whole of other income as IRA money.
  The two Over Time sections have one, and already treat other income as
  separate from it. The prose in both places now says which is which rather
  than leaving the reader to notice they differ.
- **It counts toward the RMD.** `taxableRmd = max(0, rmd - qcd)` is the whole
  point of the section — a gift at least as large as the requirement satisfies
  it and reaches the return as nothing. The account gives up `max(rmd, qcd)`,
  not the sum.
- **It compounds.** Every later requirement is measured against a balance the
  gift already shrank, so the effect runs the same way the torpedo does, in
  reverse. That is why the projection prices it over the horizon (a second
  `projectYears` run with `annualQcd: 0`, differenced on the new
  `lifetimeRealTax`) rather than in the hovered year.

### Two judgement calls worth keeping

**71, not 70.** 408(d)(8)(B)(ii) measures 70 1/2 to the day and these
projections know only a birth year: a January baby gets there in the year they
turn 70, a December one not until 71. `QCD_FIRST_CERTAIN_AGE = ceil(70.5)`
errs a year late for half of filers rather than a year early for the other
half.

**The gift is not a fourth withdrawal order.** Money given away is money the
household no longer has, so scoring "give from the IRA" against three orders
that keep it would hand it the lowest lifetime tax for the one reason that has
nothing to do with sequencing — the same gaming `deferredTraditionalTax`
exists to stop. All three make the same gift, it stays a constant outflow, and
what varies between them is only what the exclusion is worth against each
one's ordinary income.

Except that it does not always stay constant, which turned out to be the
interesting case: the gift can only come from an IRA that still has it, and
bracket filling exists to empty that IRA early. At the default $100,000
balance the two want the same dollars and the three orders give three
different totals — $15,762 against $122,720 over twenty years. `qcdCurtailed`
detects it and the section says so, because an order that looks cheapest
partly because it gave the least is not a reading.

### The limit indexes on its own rule

408(d)(8)(A) rounds to the *nearest* $1,000; IRC 1(f) rounds the *increase*
down to $50. Both the step and the direction differ, and the published figures
bear it out ($105k / $108k / $111k for 2024-26), so `projectQcdLimit` does not
reuse the bracket indexing. It anchors on the latest published year at or
below the projected one, like everything else since 4fb01c0 — which matters
more here than it looks, since a gift growing at inflation against a limit
held flat would start getting clipped partway through the horizon for no
reason in the statute. SECURE 2.0 section 307 indexed it precisely so it would
not.

### Logged as discovered work

The sequencing section now makes the gift in all three orders and reports the
totals given, but never prices what the gift is *worth* to each one — the
projection beside it does exactly that by differencing two runs. Three more
simulations would answer the question the section is actually for: does a
recurring QCD change which order wins? Bracket filling is the one to check.

### Not verified

No visual check; the repo has no browser tooling. The two new prose blocks are
asserted on in App.test.tsx through every branch (no gift, gift priced, no
balance, too young, constant, curtailed), but their layout is argued rather
than seen.


