## 2026-08-21 — Task: Give every step the same shape

chart → the one slider that says where on it you are → the collapsed
explainers → the box to the next step. Steps 2 and 3 now read that way; step 1
has no curve of its own, so the return it sets (year, filing status, age)
stands where the chart stands and the benefit slider follows it.

### What moved

- **Step 2.** The Other Ordinary Income slider went from above the chart to
  below it — under the axis label and the IRMAA key, so the chart and its two
  captions stay one block. Nothing else in the step changed order.
- **Step 3.** Was heading → prose → chart → axis label → prose, with no slider
  at all. Now heading → one-line intro → chart → axis label → the LTCG slider →
  a single `<details className="explainer">` ("Why the two effects stack")
  holding both of the old paragraphs verbatim. The intro is written to match
  step 2's ("The chart prices every … the slider says which one is yours").
- **The LTCG slider left the advanced disclosure.** Last iteration deliberately
  left it there, reasoning that the capital-gains rework three bullets down
  would move it and moving it twice was worse. But that rework changes what the
  slider *means* (a share of income already entered rather than an addition),
  not where it lives, and step 3 cannot have the shared shape without it. So it
  moved once, to its final home.

### Why the readout exists

Moving the slider under the chart exposed something the ordering had been
hiding: `plannedLtcg` fed nothing at all. Not the torpedo curve, not the gains
curve, not a tooltip — only its own value and the advanced summary line. It had
been wired to the Roth and IRMAA sections deleted two iterations ago. Putting a
dead control in the main flow, where the disclosure's own stated rule is that
anything visible must earn its place, was not acceptable.

So each of the two charted steps got a `.slider-readout` under its slider,
reading the drawn curve back at the reader's value: "You are here. At $30,000
of other income the next dollar is taxed at 22.2%." `pointAt(curve, axis,
value)` walks the ascending sweep for the last sample at or below the value —
sliders step $500, curves sample $250, so it is an exact hit in practice and
the `undefined` branch renders an em dash. Step 3's line adds a clause when the
rate is past 20%: nothing above the top statutory LTCG rate can be the gain
itself, so the excess is benefit being dragged in beside it.

This is prose where the next backlog item asks for a labelled dashed vertical.
They complement rather than duplicate — the line puts the reader on the chart,
the sentence says what it costs them there — and the item stays open.

### Also

- `advancedSet` is two entries now, so "All three at $0" → "Both at $0" and
  the field note lost its capital-gains clause.
- The QCD field note said the gift comes out of "the other income set above".
  That slider has been *below* the disclosure since the tabs became steps —
  stale before this change, not caused by it. Now reads "set in step 2", which
  survives the next reordering too.

### CSS

`.chart-slider` (a top margin, no bottom one — it reads as part of the chart,
not as a control in a stack) and `.slider-readout` / `.slider-readout strong`,
sized like a field note.

