## 2026-08-21 — Task: Draw a labelled dashed vertical at the reader's position

Struck two bullets with one change: the Backlog-2 item and the "you are here"
ReferenceLine bullet in Discovered Work, which the backlog item itself said to
do as one.

Last iteration gave each slider a `.slider-readout` sentence reading the curve
back at the reader's value. This puts the same fact on the chart: a dashed
vertical at the slider's own x, labelled `You are here`. Prose says what the
point costs, the line says where it is.

### `hereLine(value, axisMax, colour)`

A plain function returning a `<ReferenceLine>`, not a component — recharts
identifies its children by element type, so a wrapper renders as an unknown
child and is dropped. Same reason `cliffsOnChart.map` sits inline.

Three things it settles:

- **Colour** is the driving slider's, not the curve's: amber (#f59e0b) on step
  2, emerald (#34d399) on step 3. So each marker matches the control under it
  rather than the line beside it, and the readout can say "the dashed amber
  line" and be followed. A test pins the two strokes to that prose.
- **Dash** is `6 4` at strokeWidth 2, against the IRMAA cliffs' `4 4` at 1 —
  the two dashed verticals on step 2 have to read as different kinds of thing.
- **Label side** flips at 60% of the axis. `insideTopLeft`/`insideTopRight` are
  named for a rectangle; a reference line is a rectangle of zero width, so on
  one they mean "text to the right of the line" and "text to the left of it"
  (recharts' getCartesianPosition: `upperX + offset`, anchor `start` vs
  `upperX + 0 - offset`, anchor `end`). Without the flip the label runs off the
  right edge at high incomes.

Label goes *inside* the plot, not above it: `position: 'top'` is already the
IRMAA labels', and stacking a fourth label in that strip guarantees a
collision. Inside-top lands at plot-top + 5, clear of them. It can overlap the
curve when the reader stands on the torpedo peak, but labels render in
recharts' z-index-2000 layer, above every series, so it stays legible.

### Prose

Both readouts now name the line rather than gesturing at "that point on the
curve above". Kept the "At $30,000 of other income the next dollar is taxed at
X%" clause verbatim — two existing tests assert on it, and it is still the
sentence's job.

### Tests

`App.chart.test.tsx`, new `describe('the "you are here" marker')`, 4 cases:
one labelled marker per chart with the right two strokes; each marker tracks
its own slider and only its own (moving other income leaves the gains marker
where it was); the marker sits at the reader's own fraction of a linear axis
($75k is exactly halfway between the $0 and $150k positions); the label flips
`text-anchor` from `start` to `end` past mid-axis.

Two gotchas worth keeping:

- The cliff-position helper had to be re-scoped. Its selector was every
  `.recharts-reference-line-line` in the first chart, which now counts the
  marker as a fourth cliff — `:not(.here-line)` fixes it. `className` on a
  ReferenceLine lands on the wrapping Layer, not on the `<line>`.
- Labels are **not** inside the reference line that owns them. Recharts lifts
  every label into one `g.recharts-zIndex-layer_2000` at the root of the SVG,
  so a marker's label is found by its words within a `.recharts-wrapper`, not
  by its line. Cost a probe run to discover.

### Verified

`npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run` (7 files,
416 tests, green in 4.0s), `npm run build`. No CSS change — the marker is all
SVG attributes. JS bundle 554.28 kB. No headless browser in the project, so
the geometry is asserted in jsdom rather than seen; that is what the fraction
and anchor tests are for.

## 2026-08-21 — Task: Re-do the capital gains step as a share of income

Struck two bullets: the Backlog-2 item and the `plannedLtcg`-reaches-no-chart
bullet in Discovered Work. They were the same change seen from two sides — the
backlog item's "audit every caller, ordinary becomes X − LTCG" *is* the
threading the Discovered Work bullet asked for.

The task wording predates two iterations: step 3's own ordinary-income slider
and the LTCG slider's place in the advanced disclosure were both gone already,
and `totalTaxWithLTCG` is now `totalTax(scenario)`. What was left is the whole
of it: a gain stops being additive.

### The model

The reader's non-Social-Security income is **one number**, and its composition
is **one more number**. Step 2 sweeps the total holding the split; step 3
sweeps the split holding the total. Two perpendicular cuts through the same
scenario, meeting at the reader's own point — where both charts now report the
same total tax ($543 at the defaults with a $20,000 gain, checked both ways).

`splitOtherIncome(otherIncome, ltcg)` in tax.ts is the whole of the arithmetic:
`{ ordinaryIncome: X − g, ltcg: g }`, with `g` clamped to `X` because there is
no $10,000 gain inside $5,000 of income.

Both sweeps take a `gainsWithinIncome` flag, off by default. Off is the
statute's reading — ordinary income and gains are separate line items and
nothing stops a filer having both — which is why every existing tax.test.ts
case stayed green untouched. On is what a chart wants when the reader has been
asked for one income figure and then asked how much of it is gain.

`otherIncomeAtIrmaaMagi` needed no flag: it solves on the chart's axis, so it
just honours the scenario's `ltcg` as a share. No caller passed one before.

### What changed on screen

- **Step 2's curve now honours the gain**, which changes its shape rather than
  its labels. At $60,000 with a $20,000 gain the next dollar costs 27%, not
  22% — the ordinary dollar lifts the gain stack across the 0%/15% line. The
  stacking effect, showing up on the torpedo chart. At $30,000 it runs the
  other way: 22.2% → 18.5%.
- **Step 3's axis ends at the reader's own income**, and `MAX_LTCG` is gone —
  there is nothing left for a constant to bound. The slider's range labels read
  "None of it" / "All $30,000 of it".
- **A new readout sentence** prices the split against the all-ordinary version
  of the same income: at the defaults, $20,000 of the $30,000 taken as gain
  saves $2,270. That figure is the point of the whole reframing and neither
  chart shows it — both ends are points on the gains curve, so it costs no
  extra arithmetic.
- **Pulling the income slider under a gain drags the gain with it**, the same
  re-cap the charitable gift already got when its limit falls.
- **An empty state at $0 of other income**: the axis has no width, the slider
  no travel and the curve one point, so say "nothing to split yet" rather than
  draw it.

### Two consequences worth knowing

**IRMAA got simpler, not harder.** The Discovered Work bullet worried that
"IRMAA MAGI is AGI, and AGI includes capital gains, so the cliffs are the wrong
ones by the full amount of the gain". Under the non-additive model that
dissolves: AGI counts both halves at face value and so does provisional income,
so MAGI — and every cliff on the axis — is blind to the split. The complaint
was an artefact of gains being additive.

**Except through the charitable gift.** `qcdFor` caps the exclusion by the
ordinary income there is to exclude it from, and a gain is a sale, not a
distribution. So a large enough gain crowds the gift out and *does* move the
cliffs left. That is the one place the split reaches MAGI, it has a test, and
the field note under the QCD slider now says so.

### Tests

+18 cases, 416 → 434.

tax.test.ts, new `describe('gains carved out of income rather than stacked on
top')`: `splitOtherIncome` (carve, clamp, never negative); the two sweeps under
the flag, each pinned against a hand-built `totalTax` call at the same point,
and each shown to differ from the additive default; the taxable benefit
unchanged across the whole gains axis (the property that makes that chart
readable); total tax non-increasing as the gain share grows — true because the
preferential rate is below the ordinary one at every position in the stack, and
qualified with why a QCD breaks it; and the two IRMAA cases above.

App.test.tsx, new `describe('a gain is a share of the income, not an addition
to it')`: the axis tracks the income slider, the gain gets dragged down, the
torpedo readout re-prices (22.2% → 18.5%), the saving sentence reads $2,270,
and the $0 empty state. Renamed selectors across both test files for the two
new slider labels — "Other Income (not Social Security)" and "Long-Term Capital
Gains Inside That Income".

### Verified

`npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run` (7 files, 434
tests, green in 4.4s), `npm run build`. No CSS change — the empty state reuses
`.step-prose`.

### Logged, not fixed

Two new Discovered Work bullets: step 3's curve is still drawn in step 2's
amber, which reads as "same chart rescaled" when the two are now perpendicular
sweeps; and the advanced-inputs summary line reports the charitable gift as set
rather than as allowed, which a large gain can now cut into.

================================================================
Re-scale the chart x-axes so the features worth seeing fit
================================================================

The complaint was specific: `MAX_INCOME = 150_000` cut the senior deduction's
phaseout in half. The phaseout ends at $175,000 of MAGI unmarried and $250,000
joint, which is $154,845 and $229,845 of *other* income once a typical benefit
is in AGI — so the second hump the explainer spends four paragraphs on had no
right-hand side on the chart, and the prose had to say "sits past the right
edge of the chart" instead.

### What the axis is now

`incomeAxisMax(scenario, { minimum })` in tax.ts. It ends a little past the
last thing that happens on the curve:

  max(MIN_INCOME_AXIS, lastFeature * 1.05), rounded up to $25,000

where the features are the two humps' right-hand *feet* — past them the curve
is a flat rate schedule that says nothing new:

- `otherIncomeAtTaxableSSCap` — where 85% of the benefit is in the tax base and
  no further dollar can drag in a cent more. $40,562 single, $48,797 joint,
  $11,856 (half the benefit) on a separate return.
- `otherIncomeAtAgi(phaseoutEnd)` — the senior deduction's far side, null when
  there is no deduction to phase out (under 65, separate return, year outside
  2025-2028), because then there is no second hump to make room for.

Both invert a monotone income definition by bisection, sharing one private
`otherIncomeAt` helper with `otherIncomeAtIrmaaMagi`'s existing approach.

Result, at the average benefit: $150,000 for anyone under 65 (unchanged),
$175,000 single/HOH 65+, $250,000 joint 65+.

### The floor is the interesting decision

I first floored it at $100,000 and let the axis *shrink* for a filer under 65 —
the torpedo would have filled 41% of the plot rather than 27%. Backed it out.
The slider under the chart shares the axis's right edge, so narrowing the axis
takes away the reader's reach: a joint filer could no longer model $130,000 of
income. And $150,000 is what puts an unmarried return's first three IRMAA
cliffs on screen. The task's own wording treats both as things $150,000 gets
right. So `MIN_INCOME_AXIS = 150_000` and the axis only ever grows. Logged the
road not taken under Discovered Work.

The other floor: App passes `minimum: max(MIN_INCOME_AXIS, ordinaryIncome)`, so
the axis can never pull in behind the reader's own marker. Without it, ticking
65+, sliding out to $170,000 and unticking would strand the marker past the
edge.

### What changed on screen

- **A joint return claiming the senior deduction now sees its IRMAA cliffs.**
  Its tier-1 cliff is at $196,876 of other income; on the old axis nothing was
  drawn and the reader got the fallback sentence. The axis stretching to
  $250,000 for the phaseout brings the cliff along. (Tier 2 at $252,876 misses
  by $2,876 — cliffs ride along, they do not steer.)
- **The explainer's caveat flips**: "sits past the right edge of the chart"
  becomes "is inside the chart" the moment the deduction is claimed, which is
  true by construction now.
- **The QCD slider and the "you are here" marker** follow the same edge, and
  `curveStep` halves to $500 past $150,000 so the widest chart samples no more
  points than the narrowest always did.

### Tests

+21, 434 -> 455.

tax.test.ts, new `describe('sizing the income axis to the return')`: the cap
inverter pinned against hand arithmetic and against `taxableSocialSecurity`
either side of the answer, per filing status, at zero benefit, and moving
