at nothing. It is honest — that run is exactly what the gift buys — but it may
be a worse teacher than a sentence would be. Options are capping the axis and
marking the gift off-chart the way the IRMAA cliffs already are, breaking the
axis, or leaving it.

Note also that the existing "a gain crowds out the gift" bullet gets more
reachable with this change: the summary line still names the gift the reader
set, not the smaller amount their income can actually exclude, and the gift can
now be set well past any income.


Tell the reader which way to move, not just where they are
==========================================================

The tooltip has carried this arithmetic since the third iteration — "stay under
$x or over $y" on a hill, "fill this valley" in a valley — but only for
whichever point the mouse happened to be over. That is nobody's point in
particular, and no point at all on a touchscreen. The slider knows the one
point worth saying it about.

### The classifier

`standingOn(segments, x)` in tax.ts, next to the `segmentCurve` it reads:

    kind: 'valley' | 'climbing' | 'peak' | 'past' | 'flat'
    here / prev / next   the stretch the reader is in and its neighbours
    hump                 the hill in play
    cheaperBehind        the nearest stretch below them charging less

The one rule that needed deciding: **the hump ahead beats the hump behind.**
The dip between the Social Security torpedo and the senior deduction's
phaseout is shaped exactly like the dip past the last hump, and `segmentCurve`
types both of them 'valley' — but a reader in the first has a climb coming and
a reader in the second does not, so the classifier looks forward first and only
then back. That is why 'past' can be returned for a segment whose own type is
'valley'.

`cheaperBehind` is the *nearest* cheaper stretch, not the cheapest. The
cheapest is almost always the run below the standard deduction, which is true
and no use to anyone deciding whether to hold a withdrawal back a year. Nearest
gives the 2025 single filer at $60,000 the 12% valley at $40,750–$44,000 — the
one they just cleared and could get back to.

That field came out of a wrong sentence rather than a plan. The first draft of
the 'past' branch read "holding income back only helps if it lands in a year
that starts below $22,750", naming the near edge of the hump. False for exactly
the reader most likely to read it: at $60,000 a year starting in the 12% valley
is cheaper too, and the hump's near edge is nowhere near the boundary that
matters.

### The prose

`StandingNote` in App.tsx, one branch per kind, rendered under the readout in
step 2's slider group. Every branch names dollars and rates the reader can act
on and never names the mechanism — "the dearest stretch on this chart", not
"the 85% inclusion cap" — because the same hump shape is also drawn by the
senior deduction phaseout, and the mechanism is what the explainers below the
chart are for. The default page load ($30,000, single, average benefit) opens
mid-hump, which is the case the whole app exists to show:

> **You are standing on the hump.** The next dollar costs 22.2% — the highest
> rate this chart reaches, and it holds from $22,750 to $40,500. Coming back
> under $22,750 — $7,250 less income — takes the next dollar down to 18.5%;
> clearing $40,750 — $10,750 more — takes it to 12%. Every dollar in between is
> charged the hump rate, so the move that pays is around this stretch rather
> than into it: stop short of the near edge, or take enough at once to land
> past the far one.

Guards that earn their keep: a reader standing exactly on the near edge drops
the "$0 less income" clause; a hump that starts at the first dollar (no `prev`)
says so instead of offering a way off to the left. The second is unreachable
today — even the separate return with $0 thresholds has a standard deduction
under it — and stays in because a future input could take it away.

