Say what the page is for, not what its controls are set to
==========================================================

The h1 read "Marginal Tax Rate" and the subtitle under it read "Federal
marginal rate on the next dollar of other income for a single filer (2025
brackets, standard deduction), with Social Security taxed under the 50%/85%
provisional-income rules." Accurate, and a readout of two controls the reader
had not scrolled to yet. The first thing on the page was the app describing its
own state.

### The hero

    How Much Can You Take Out This Year?

    One more dollar out of an IRA — a withdrawal, a Roth conversion, a
    realized gain — can drag Social Security into the tax base with it and
    shove a long-term gain out of the 0% band, so what the next dollar
    actually costs is often nothing like your bracket. This draws that cost
    across every income level for your own return, and marks the stretches
    worth filling and the ones worth stepping around.

The title is the question a reader arrives with, phrased as the decision they
are there to make rather than as the quantity the chart plots. The subtitle
names the three moves that ask it (withdrawal, conversion, sale), the two
mechanisms that make the answer surprising, and — in the last clause — what the
page now does about it, which is the `standingOn` advice the previous iteration
landed. No filing status, no year, no benefit: nothing in the hero changes when
a control moves.

`index.html` got the same treatment. Its `<title>` and description had the same
"see your federal marginal tax rate across income levels" framing, and the
description is what a search result shows, so it now names the four things the
chart draws (torpedo, gain stacking, IRMAA cliffs, senior-deduction phaseout).

### Where the state went

Deleting the status and the year from the hero would have deleted them from the
page — nothing else says which return is being priced in one place. They moved
to a recap line at the foot of step 1, after the advanced disclosure and
immediately before the next-step box:

> Everything from here on prices one return: **2025** brackets and standard
> deduction, **a single filer**, under 65, collecting **$23,712** of Social
> Security a year.

That is the right place for it twice over: it is the last thing in the step
that sets those values, and the first thing a reader passes on the way into the
step that spends them. `ageProse` handles the one case with three answers
rather than two — a joint return with one qualifying spouse is not the same
return as one with two, since the senior deduction and the standard-deduction
addition each apply per person. A $0 benefit reads "collecting no Social
Security at all" rather than "$0 of Social Security a year", and the sentence
gains "Plus whatever is set under Advanced inputs above." only when muni
interest or a charitable gift has been moved off zero — the recap names three
settings and must not imply it names five.

### Tests

+7, 479 -> 486. The hero test asserts the new heading and the subtitle's
subject matter, then asserts the negative that motivated the whole change:
switching to a joint 2026 return leaves the subtitle untouched. The recap gets
its own describe — placement (inside `#step-benefit`, immediately before
`.next-step`), the default sentence in full, following year/status/benefit
together, one senior versus two, the $0-benefit wording, and the advanced
clause appearing and disappearing.

Three existing assertions read the old subtitle for the year ("2025 brackets,
standard deduction") and were re-pointed at the recap. They cannot use
`getByText` for the figure any more — the year sits in its own `<strong>`, and
`getNodeText` only joins an element's direct text children — so a shared
`scenarioRecap()` helper finds the line by its opening phrase and the
assertions run through `toHaveTextContent`, which reads the whole subtree. The
head-of-household test that counts two occurrences of "a head of household"
still counts two; the second one is the recap now instead of the subtitle, and
its comment says so.

### Verified

`npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run` (7 files, 486
tests, green in 5.1s), `npm run build`. CSS: `.scenario-recap` (a left rule in
the step's own sky blue, matching `.slider-advice`'s treatment) and a 62ch
`max-width` on `.subtitle`, which is now three lines instead of two.


Research pass: what the three-step app is still missing
=======================================================

Backlog 2 emptied last iteration, so the topmost unchecked bullet was the
standing "Research and create more action items." No code changed. Baseline
confirmed green first — `npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`,
`npx vitest run` (7 files, 486 tests, 5.2s) — so the backlog is written against
a working tree, not a guess.

### What the app is now

Three mounted sections on one scrolling page. Step 1 sets the return (year,
filing status, age, benefit, plus muni interest and a charitable gift behind an
Advanced disclosure) and closes on the scenario recap. Step 2 is the torpedo:
marginal rate against other income, IRMAA cliff lines, the reader's own dashed
vertical, the slider, `StandingNote`, four explainers. Step 3 sweeps the split
rather than the size — how much of step 2's income is a long-term gain — with
its own chart, slider and one explainer. Then the disclaimer.

### The finding that reordered the list

`conversionCeilings`, `maxConversionUnder` and `sizeConversion` are complete,
documented, tested, and imported by nothing. `ConversionSizing` returns the
largest conversion under a chosen ceiling, its tax, the average cost per dollar
and the rate past the ceiling. Meanwhile the h1 reads "How Much Can You Take
Out This Year?" — that exact question — and the page answers it with a curve and
a sentence of advice. The app has the number and does not show it. That went to
the top.

Second: `point.totalTax` appears twice in App.tsx and both are inside a
tooltip. Every figure on the page is a *rate*. A reader who does not hover
never sees a dollar of tax, and there is no effective rate anywhere at all.

Third: both `chart-container` divs are bare recharts SVG — no `role`, no
`aria-label`, no caption. All the prose is positioned relative to the reader's
own point, so nothing on the page ever describes the curve's shape. `segments`
is that shape as data already, which makes the caption cheap.

Also logged: no query-string state (ten `useState` values, so a refresh loses
the scenario and there is nothing to send an advisor), no closing recap to
mirror step 1's opening one, `stateTax.ts` unread by any reader while the page
says only "federal income tax only", and a README/package name still calling
this "Growth Projector".

### Triage, not just addition

Discovered Work had grown to fifteen unchecked bullets, four of which are
polish on code that renders nowhere — the sequencing QCD pricing, the
nominal-dollar shortfall sum, the COLA anchoring question and the 2026 state
figures were all written while the Over Time and State Taxes tabs still
existed. A note at the head of that section says so and names them, so a future
iteration does not spend a pass improving something no reader can reach. They
stay unchecked: each is a real defect, and each returns with its section.

Backlog 3 ends with another "Research and create more action items." so the
loop can regenerate when these run out.


Answer the h1 with a number: the conversion sizer returns as step 4
===================================================================

`conversionCeilings`, `maxConversionUnder` and `sizeConversion` have been
complete, documented and tested since before the Strategies tab was deleted in
5fe9854, and have rendered nowhere since. Meanwhile the h1 asks "How Much Can
You Take Out This Year?" and the page answered with a curve and a sentence of
advice. Step 4 answers it with a dollar figure.

### The shape

Same as the other three, with one substitution. Chart, then the one control
that moves the reader along it, then explainers, then the close — except the
control is a radio group rather than a slider, because the lines a conversion
is sized against are six named places rather than a continuum. `landmarks()` in
the shared-shape test now reports 'control' where it reported 'slider', which
is the honest generalisation: the slot is about what moves the marker, not
about which widget does it. Step 3 gained the next-step box it never had, and
step 4 is last so it has none.

### The chart is step 2's

A ceiling is quoted in taxable income, provisional income or MAGI. The chart's
axis is none of the three. What ties them together is the conversion itself:
measured in other income from where the reader already stands, it *is* the
distance to the line — so drawing the band from `ordinaryIncome` out to
`ordinaryIncome + sizing.conversion` puts the ceiling on the axis, and the
dashed line closing the band is the ceiling. That is also why the band is
visibly shorter than the headroom the line appears to offer: every dollar
inside the torpedo raises taxable income by more than a dollar. Default return,
2025: $23,047 of headroom under the top of the 12% bracket, $14,069 of
conversion.

The axis is `incomeAxisMax(..., { minimum: Math.max(axisMax, conversionTarget) })`,
so it never narrows below step 2's — the two charts are the same sweep and a
reader comparing them should not have to re-read the axis — and widens when the
conversion runs past it. A joint return sized to the top of the 22% bracket
converts $188,044 and needs $225,000 of axis. When the two axes agree,
`conversionCurve` *is* `curve`, not a copy of it; `curveStep` came out to a
module-level `curveStepFor` so both charts sample by the same rule.

Its own `conversionSegments` rather than step 2's, so the tooltip still finds a
hill or valley over the stretch only this chart draws.

The label went on the ceiling line rather than inside the band: "You are here"
runs rightwards from the band's near edge, and a narrow band would have stacked
the two. Above the axis is free here because step 4 draws no IRMAA cliffs.

### What the readout says

Three branches. The conversion that fits, priced: the amount, the tax it costs,
the year's bill before and after, the average cost per dollar, and the rate on
the first dollar past the line — 19.65% averaged against 22% marginal, on the
default return, which is the whole argument for sizing to a line rather than to
a bracket rate. Then `alreadyOver`, which says how far past the line the return
already is rather than "$0 fits" (the 50% base is $16,856 behind the default
reader). Then the sub-dollar case, for completeness.

`ceiling.note` carries the "past the line" advice, so the six ceilings' own
prose — written with the arithmetic, and already covering the separate-return
collapse of both SS bases onto $0 — reaches the page unedited.

### Scenario translation

Steps 2 and 3 treat the gain as a share of one other-income figure; the tax
chain treats the two as separate line items. `sizeConversion` reads the second
shape, so `conversionScenario` runs `splitOtherIncome` first. Without it a
conversion would land on a total that is part gain, and a gain is not
convertible.

### Tests

+14, 486 -> 500. Nine in a new `sizing the conversion` describe (the priced
answer with every figure, the six captioned ceilings, re-sizing on a new line,
the already-over sentence, re-pricing when step 2's slider moves, the note, the
explainer list, the widening axis with step 2's slider left alone, and the
band's far edge in other-income terms). Five in `App.chart.test.tsx` for the
band's geometry: near edge on the reader's marker, far edge on the ceiling
line, the label, growth on a further-out line, nothing drawn when nothing fits,
and both edges sliding with step 2's income. That file does not pin the clock,
so none of its assertions name a dollar.

Eight existing tests moved from three steps to four; `hereLabels`/
`herePositions` now see three charts, and step 4's marker is amber like step
2's because it stands on the same axis at the same figure.

### Verified

`npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run` (7 files, 500
tests, green in 5.4s), `npm run build`. CSS: `.segmented-stacked` for a picker
whose six options each carry a caption, `.ceiling-picker` as a fieldset
standing in for an `.input-group` (deliberately not `.filing-status`, whose
element-plus-class `margin: 0` `.chart-slider` cannot win back), and an indigo
band/swatch/advice rule — the one accent no curve, cliff or marker already
owned.


Say what the return owes, not only what the next dollar costs
=============================================================

Every rate the page quoted was the price of the next dollar. The total bill
existed only inside `CustomTooltip` and `LTCGTooltip`, so a reader who never
hovered — and every reader on a touch screen — walked all four steps without
once being told what the return costs. Both "You are here" readouts now say it,
and say the effective rate beside it, which nothing on the page stated at all.

### One figure, three places

`herePoint.totalTax` and `hereGainPoint.totalTax` are the same number: the two
charts sweep one return two ways and the reader stands at the same return on
each. `sizeConversion` rounds the same `totalTax` call into `taxBefore`, so
step 4's "this year's bill" is the third copy. Default return, 2025: $2,813 in
all three. Each readout still reads its own curve rather than sharing a value,
which is the pattern the marginal rates already follow — the claim that they
agree is left out of the prose, because a curve sampled at $1,000 steps and a
slider left at a $500 multiple could in principle disagree.

### The denominator

The page already defines total income, in the axis label under step 2's chart:
other income + the whole benefit + tax-exempt interest − anything given
straight to charity. The effective rate uses that, not taxable income. Against
taxable income the untaxed benefit vanishes from the comparison, and the
untaxed benefit is the thing the page is about.

Three consequences, all tested. Muni interest is money received, so it lands in
the denominator ($10,000 of it takes the default return to $63,712 and the rate
to 6.02% on a bill of $3,833). A gift leaves the IRA without reaching the
filer, so it comes out ($90,000 of income less a $20,000 gift is $93,712). And
only the gift 408(d)(8) can actually exclude comes out — `qcdFor`, not the
slider's own value — so at $90,000 with $80,000 of it a gain, $10,000 of the
gift is crowded out and counts back in, giving $103,712.

That last one is where the readout is now right and the axis label above it is
wrong: the label subtracts the asked-for gift. Logged in Discovered work,
alongside the older disagreement it belongs with — both tooltips compute total
income as `income + ssBenefit`, ignoring muni and the gift, so the tooltip and
the sentence below it now differ by the muni whenever it is set. Neither was
introduced here; the new sentence is what makes them visible.

### The wording

Step 2: "The return itself owes $2,813 in federal tax on $53,712 of total
income — an effective rate of 5.24%. That is the average across every dollar of
it; the figure before it is the price of the next one." The last clause is the
whole reason the two figures share a sentence — a marginal rate read as an
average is the standard misreading, and here they are 22.2% and 5.24%.

No comparison is asserted between them. Effective is usually the smaller, but
just past a big torpedo drop the marginal rate falls back to 12% while the
average is still carrying the 22.2% stretch behind it, so "well under" would be
a claim the page cannot make.

Step 3: "All told the return owes $2,813 in federal tax on the $53,712 of total
income this slider never moves — an effective rate of 5.24%. The same dollars,
taxed differently: what the slider moves is the bill, not the income." Step 3's
axis is the composition of an income whose size it never changes, so the
denominator is the constant and the numerator is what moves. At $20,000 of
gain: $543 and 1.01%.

Both clauses are suppressed when nothing comes in — no benefit, no income —
where "owes $0 on $0 of total income" would be the only thing they could say.

### Two small tidyings

`conversionScenario` was the reader's return in the shape the tax chain reads,
built for step 4 and needed again for `qcdFor`. Renamed `hereScenario`, moved up
beside `herePoint`, and step 4 now reads it rather than owning it.

The joining space between step 3's marginal-rate clause and the mix-saving
sentence moved inside the two `mixSaving` branches. It was rendering as a
double space whenever `mixSaving` was null, which is every return with no gain
set — i.e. the default one — now that a third clause follows it.

### Verified

`npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run` (7 files, 510
tests, green in 8s), `npm run build`. +10 tests in a new `the total the return
owes` describe: the bill and rate under each slider, the average/marginal
wording, step 4's third copy of the total, re-pricing on the gains slider with
the denominator held, all three figures moving with the income, the muni and
gift denominators, the crowded-out gift, and the silent case. No CSS: both
clauses are prose inside `.slider-readout`.


Say what the picture says, in words
===================================

Three charts, three bare recharts SVGs: no `role`, no `aria-label`, no
caption. A screen reader got nothing from the app's centrepiece, and every
word the page spent on the curve — the tooltips, `StandingNote`, both slider
readouts — was relative to wherever the reader's own slider happened to be.
Nothing described the curve. Each chart is now a `<figure>` whose contents are
the plot, its axis label, its key and a caption that reads the curve out band
by band; the plot itself is `role="img"` with a name and an
`aria-describedby` pointing at that caption.

### The caption

`segmentCurve` has carried the shape all along — it is what the tooltip reads
to say "stay under $x or over $y" — so the caption is a rendering of data that
was already there. Two sentences: the bands, then the humps.

2025, single, the $23,712 average benefit: "Left to right, the rate on the
next dollar of other income is 0% up to $14,750, 15% to $22,000, 18.5% to
$22,500, 22.2% to $40,500, 12% to $44,000, 22% to $98,750, then 24% out to
$150,000. The hump is the 22.2% stretch between $22,750 and $40,500; the
ground just past it is cheaper, which is what makes it worth stepping over
rather than into."

That second sentence is the thing the page has never stated for any reader:
where the torpedo starts and stops, without first putting a slider inside it.
Visible rather than screen-reader-only, because reading a band edge off a
compact axis is guesswork for a sighted reader too.

Each band is named by its own last sampled point, so consecutive bands quote
figures one sampling step apart ($250 on the narrowest chart) rather than a
shared edge. That is the approximation `StandingNote` already makes every time
it quotes `hump.start` and `hump.end`.

### What the hump sentence is allowed to claim

`segmentCurve` marks a `hill` when a stretch is dearer than both neighbours —
or, at an edge, dearer than the one neighbour it has. The gains curve humps
against its own left edge routinely (10.2% from $0), so "costs more than the
ground on both sides" would be false there. The claim made instead is the one
the classification guarantees everywhere: the ground just past it is cheaper.

Three branches, all tested. No hills: "No stretch of it is a hump: none costs
more than the ground on both sides of it" — a return with no benefit to drag
in. One: named outright. More than one: "It humps twice: 22.2% between $44,500
and $60,500, and 26.88% between $202,500 and $209,500" — the inclusion phase
and the senior deduction's phaseout on a joint return with both spouses 65+,
and the second is the one no reader would guess was there. The hump is left
unnamed for the same reason `StandingNote` leaves it unnamed: two mechanisms
draw the same shape, and the explainers below the chart are where mechanism
belongs.

A one-band curve — "a flat 15% the whole way, from $0 to $90,000", which is
step 3 past the torpedo — gets no hump sentence at all. Being one band has
already said it.

### The labels

Each `role="img"` carries its own axis and right edge rather than a shared
string: step 2's is other income out to `axisMax`, step 3's is "as more of
$30,000 of other income is taken as long-term capital gain", and step 4's says
it is step 2's curve redrawn, and where the conversion is shaded — or that
nothing fits under the line and no band is drawn, which is the one thing on
that chart a caption of bands cannot say.

Step 4's caption repeats step 2's whenever the axes match, which is the common
case. Deliberate — a figure should stand alone for a reader who lands on step
4 — but logged in Discovered work as a call worth settling once.

### Verified

`npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run` (7 files,
522 tests, green in 8.4s), `npm run build`. +12 tests in a new `the curve in
words` describe: the figure/role/describedby wiring on all three, step 2's
full band run, the hump sentence, that the caption does *not* move when the
slider does, the no-hump and two-hump returns, step 3's own wording, the flat
curve, step 3's figure vanishing with its axis, step 4's lead, and each
label. CSS: `.chart-figure` is a margin reset (`<figure>` ships with 40px) and
`.chart-caption` is `.chart-key`'s size, colour and 70px plot-area offset,
since it is the same kind of thing — a note about the picture rather than part
of it.

`bandRun` and `humpNote` are module-private: exporting them tripped
`react-refresh/only-export-components`, and the page's prose has always been
tested through `render(<App />)` anyway.

Close the page with the reader's own answer
===========================================

Between step 4's last explainer and the disclaimer there was nothing. A reader
walked four charts and left with no single place saying what the return costs:
every figure on the page was one step's figure, quoted where that step needed
it. The page now ends in `#answer` — a card carrying the six figures a reader
actually leaves with, plus what step 4 sized.

The mirror of the recap that closes step 1. That one names what was set; this
says what came of it.

### Where it sits

Outside step 4 rather than at the foot of it, against the Discovered Work
bullet that put it there. It summarises all four steps and belongs to none of
them, so making it part of step 4's `<section>` would have put it inside the
step nav's `aria-controls` for step 4 and read as step 4's own close. Last
before the `<footer>` either way, which is what the original bullet asked for.

That decision has a cost, logged in Discovered work: nothing on the page points
at it. The nav has four buttons, `nextStepBox` returns null on the last step,
and the answer is a fifth landmark below three collapsed explainers.

### The seven figures

Total income, federal tax, effective rate, the rate on the next dollar, the
benefit in the tax base, the Medicare tier, and the room to convert. Six from
the bullet; the seventh is what the step-4 bullet added when it landed there
first.

Two of them the page has never stated for any reader. **Benefit in the tax
base** — $11,178 of $23,712, 47.14% of it, on the default return — is the slope
every curve is drawn from, and it had never once been a figure. **Medicare
surcharge** was available only by hovering the chart, which is nothing at all
on a touch screen: the close names the tier, the MAGI, the distance to the next
cliff, what crossing it costs, and the two-year lag that means 2025 income is
setting a 2027 premium.

The other five are third renderings of figures already on screen rather than
second calculations of them. `hereTax` is `herePoint.totalTax` — the same value
step 2's readout quotes and the same one step 4 calls "this year's bill", since
`sizing.taxBefore` computes it straight from `hereScenario`. A test asserts all
three agree on $2,813 and 5.24%.

### What it says when a figure would be a claim about nothing

Effective rate goes to an em dash when nothing comes in — "0.00%" on $0 of
income is a statement about a denominator that does not exist — with the reason
in its own gloss. The benefit row says "None" and names step 1 as the place
that set it. The conversion row carries all three of `sizeConversion`'s
outcomes, and both no-room branches now name the ceiling's own measure
alongside its label, because four income definitions are in that picker and
"$45,930 past Top of the 12% bracket" does not say which one it is measured in.

### The screenshot

The bullet's last clause — "the thing a reader would screenshot" — is why the
block restates the return above the figures: `Priced for 2025: a single filer,
under 65, with $23,712 of Social Security and $30,000 of other income.` An
answer cropped away from its question is worth nothing, and step 1's recap is
about 4,000 words up the page.

That repetition is what broke four existing tests. Three `getByText` calls that
meant "the step 1 recap" started matching two elements, and a count of "a head
of household" went 2 → 3. Scoped to `scenarioRecap()` and re-counted; logged in
Discovered work, because it is the second time a page-wide prose assertion has
broken on an unrelated addition.

### One tidying

`qcdFor(hereScenario)` was inlined in `totalIncome`. Named `given`, because the
close quotes the figure as well as subtracting it — and it is the *allowed*
gift, not the slider's, which is the distinction Discovered work item 84 is
still open on.

### Verified

`npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run` (7 files, 533
tests, green in 9s), `npm run build`. +11 tests in a new `the closing answer`
describe: where it sits in the document, all seven default figures, the
restated return under two statuses, the three-way agreement with steps 2 and 4,
every figure re-pricing at $90,000 of income (into IRMAA tier 1 and out of any
room to convert), the tier gloss, two enrollees on a joint return, the
no-benefit case, muni in and the gift out of the denominator, the ceiling
