Make a scenario survivable and shareable
========================================

Ten `useState` values were the whole return, so a refresh threw it away and
there was nothing to send to a spouse or an advisor. All ten now live in the
query string: read once on mount, written on every change. `src/utils/
scenarioUrl.ts` owns both directions; App.tsx keeps only the wiring.

The encoding is trivial and was never the work. The three decisions the bullet
named were.

### The step is not in the link

It is where the reader is looking, not what the return holds, and the page
already names places a better way: every step is mounted and every section
carries `id="step-…"`, so `#step-conversion` is a fragment the browser scrolls
to on its own. The query string says what the return *is*; the fragment says
where to stand. Putting the step in the query string would also have made the
one control that changes nothing about the return the one control that
rewrites the address.

So `stepFromHash` reads the fragment on mount and marks that nav button
current — otherwise a reader who followed `#step-conversion` lands on step 4
under a nav insisting they are on step 1. The page never *writes* the hash;
`goToStep` already scrolls, and writing it would double-scroll and spend a
history entry per nav click.

That decision has a consequence that had to be handled rather than discovered:
`replaceState` takes a whole URL, so writing a bare `?query` silently drops the
fragment. `scenarioUrl` rebuilds pathname + search + hash every time.

### A year this page cannot price loses, out loud

`hasPublishedParams` (already in tax.ts) decides; anything else gets
`defaultTaxYear()` plus a note. Refusing to render would trade a page priced
for the wrong year against no page at all. The year-dependent bounds then clamp
against the year it *fell back to*, not the one the link asked for — a 2027
link carrying the 2026 maximum benefit raises two notes, and the second one
names $61,296.

The year is also the one key always written. `defaultTaxYear()` follows the
wall calendar, so a link that left it out would re-price itself the January
after it was sent, which is the opposite of what a saved link is for.
Everything else is written only when it differs from what that year opens with,
so an untouched page reads `?year=2025`. The benefit is the interesting
omission: left out when it sits on the year's average, because a reader who
never moved that slider has expressed no opinion about the figure — the rule
`changeYear` already applies inside the page.

### `replaceState`, never `pushState`

A slider fires a change per notch. Pushing would spend a history entry on every
$500 of a drag and leave Back scrubbing through it rather than leaving the page.
A test drags the income slider four notches and asserts `history.length` has
not moved.

### What a link is not allowed to say

A link is the one input this app has that it did not produce itself, so every
figure is clamped on the way in against the same bound the page's own control
would have held it inside — and every clamp leaves a note naming the bound,
which is the useful half. "Held at $62,172" tells a reader the figure was the
year's maximum benefit; "we changed your link" tells them nothing. That is what
the orphaned `src/utils/format.ts` from a previous iteration was for; it is now
imported by both the module and App.tsx, which drops App's duplicate
`formatCurrency`.

One clamp is not cosmetic. `MAX_OTHER_INCOME` exists because the income slider
has no fixed right edge — the axis is sized to the return and the slider
follows it — so an unbounded link income is an unbounded `marginalRateCurve`
sweep, which is a hung tab rather than a wrong chart. $1,000,000 clears the
highest line the page draws (the top IRMAA threshold, $750,000 joint). It also
needed a fourth rung on `curveStepFor`: the existing table stopped doubling at
$300,000, which is as wide as any slider can take it, so at $1,000,000 step 2
would have swept 1,000 points against every other chart's 600. The rung is the
rule the comment already stated, extended far enough. Step 3's sweep has no
such table at all — logged in Discovered work, along with why the obvious fix
is blocked.

The notes render as a dismissible `role="status"` card under the subtitle.
Dismissible because it describes the *arrival*, not the return: it stops being
true of what is on screen the moment a control moves, and there is no honest
way to keep it current.

### Test isolation, which this feature forces

jsdom keeps one `location` per file, so without a reset the return one test
left in the address becomes the return the next test's `render(<App />)` opens
with. `setupTests.ts` now resets to `/` in a global `beforeEach`.

### Verified

`npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run` (8 files, 567
tests, green in 9.5s), `npm run build`. +34 tests: 25 in a new
`scenarioUrl.test.ts` (round trips, every clamp and its wording, the unknown
year/status/ceiling fallbacks, negatives, fractions, empty values, and the
fragment passing through `scenarioUrl`), 9 in App.test.tsx covering the page's
half — opening on a ten-value link, writing each change back, not pushing,
surviving an unmount-and-remount, the fragment marking the step and surviving a
rewrite, and the note card appearing, saying what it swapped, and dismissing.

