## Iteration: state treatment as a footnote on step 2 (21 Aug 2026)

`stateTax.ts` had 256 lines of sourced lookup table, its own test file, four
exported functions and no reader at all — the tab that used to render it went
with the Strategies tab in 5fe9854. Meanwhile the page's only admission that
states exist was one clause at the bottom of the IRMAA explainer. Nine states
taxed a Social Security benefit in 2025, so a reader in Montana was being shown
a curve that understates their own bill with nothing on screen saying so.

### Why a footnote and not a step

The data is text. Colorado runs off age bands, Connecticut caps the taxed share
at 25% of benefits *received*, Utah works through a credit rather than a
subtraction, Vermont prorates, Montana has no rule at all. There is no shared
shape to compute, which is exactly why the table was written as prose with
citations in the first place — so what it needs is a paragraph and a source
line, not a chart, not a slider and not a fifth step in a flow that is already
four long. A menu in step 1, one conditional paragraph under step 2's chart.

### The three branches

The footnote has a branch per state of knowledge, and the middle one is the
reason the design works:

1. **No state named.** Names all nine (via `statesTaxingSocialSecurity`), says
   everything on the page is federal, and — via `statesWithMovingTests` —
   names the states whose rule reads differently in the *other* year the page
   prices. That last clause is what gives step 1's year selector a reach into
   this footnote: Minnesota re-indexes, Rhode Island indexes a year behind, West
   Virginia's phase-out finishes.
2. **A state that still taxes.** Mechanism, rule, this year's income test, the
   other year's test when `stateTestDeltas` finds one, and the source.
3. **A state that has stopped.** West Virginia in 2026, and the only reason the
   selection is *not* cleared when the state falls off the year's list. A reader
   who said "West Virginia" and then moved the year is owed the sentence saying
   the phase-out finished — clearing the menu under them would throw away both
   the answer and the question. `taxesBenefitsIn` (new, one comparison) is the
   predicate that picks between 2 and 3; the menu itself is always the full
   `STATE_SS_RULES`, with a caption counting how many of them still bite in the
   selected year (9 of 9 in 2025, 8 of 9 in 2026).

### `homeState` in the link

An eleventh value in `PageScenario`, and the first one that prices nothing. It
travels anyway: a link is meant to carry the return as its reader described it,
and "I live in Montana" is part of that description even though it moves no line
on any chart.

Its decode rules are looser than a dollar figure's, deliberately. `state=CA`
gets no footnote and *no note* — California is a real state that genuinely does
not tax the benefit, so the honest answer is the same one the page's own menu
gives it, and "we could not honour your link" would be a lie. Only a string that
is not a two-letter abbreviation at all (`state=Montana`) earns a note. Matching
is through `stateSSRule`, which was already case-insensitive and trims.

### Verified

`npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run` (8 files, 579
tests, green in 9.9s), `npm run build`. +12 tests: 8 in App.test.tsx (the
opening federal paragraph and its nine names, the moving-states clause, Montana
quoted with its source, Minnesota showing both years' tests, West Virginia
surviving the year flip into the stopped-taxing branch, the menu caption
counting per year, the link writing `state=VT` and opening on `state=UT`), 3 in
scenarioUrl.test.ts and 1 in stateTax.test.ts pinning `taxesBenefitsIn` against
the list it filters.

Three items went to Discovered work: the IRMAA explainer's now-duplicate
"federal income tax only" clause, the gate bullet that still defers work on
`stateTax.ts`'s account, and the fact that the state reaches step 2 and stops —
step 4 sizes a conversion against a federal ceiling and never mentions that
Montana taxes the conversion too.

