## Iteration: the three unrendered modules get a shelf (21 Aug 2026)

`projection.ts` (636), `sequencing.ts` (800) and `lumpSum.ts` (409) sat in
`src/utils/` beside seven modules the page actually reaches, imported by nothing
but each other and their own tests, and kept collecting Discovered Work bullets
no reader could ever see. The backlog gave two candidates: a step 5, or a
holding directory.

### Why the directory and not the step

The page is a *this-year* page. Its h1 asks "How Much Can You Take Out This
Year?" and its four steps set a scenario, walk the torpedo, split income into
gains and size a conversion — all inside one tax year, all priced off figures
the IRS has published. These three answer a different question, and to answer it
they need inputs the rest of the page never asks for: an inflation rate, a
spending need, three account balances, a birth year, and for `lumpSum.ts` a
figure for every prior year an award reaches back to. That is a second app's
worth of input hanging off one step, and a step 5 that rendered only one of the
three would leave the other two exactly as undecided as they were.

So: `src/shelf/`, six files moved with `git mv`, one `sed` rewriting `'./tax'`
to `'../utils/tax'` (the only import that crossed the new line — `sequencing.ts`
importing `./projection` and each test importing its own subject all travelled
together).

### The rule, and the test that makes it one

A directory named "shelf" is a label until something enforces it, so
`shelf.test.ts` does:

1. **Nothing outside `src/shelf/` imports from `src/shelf/`.** Walks every
   `.ts`/`.tsx` under `src/`, skips the shelf itself, and matches
   `/(^|\/)shelf\//` against each import specifier. Verified by actually
   breaking it — an `export { rmdDivisor } from '../shelf/projection'` appended
   to `format.ts` failed the test with `"utils/format.ts imports
   ../shelf/projection"`, and was reverted.
2. **The README's table is exactly what is on the shelf.** Matches only rows
   starting `| \`name\` |`, because the prose below it names `stateTax.ts` —
   which is *not* shelved — and a looser regex would read that as a fourth
   module.

Together they make un-shelving a move rather than an import: bringing a module
back means `git mv`-ing it to `src/utils/` in the same commit as the section
that renders it. The move is the decision; reversing the move is the only way to
reverse the decision.

`fileURLToPath(new URL('.', import.meta.url))` does not work here — vitest
rewrites `import.meta.url` to a non-`file:` URL and it throws "The URL must be
of scheme file". Resolved from `process.cwd()` instead, which vitest sets to the
config root.

### `src/shelf/README.md`

Table of the three with line counts and one sentence each on what they compute;
why they are here (5fe9854 was a render-layer deletion, and the page's shape
changed under them); the rule; what it would take to bring each back, ranked —
`projection.ts` first, because its thesis *is* the app's thesis, the $25k/$34k
thresholds frozen since 1984 while every benefit has moved; and the three open
defects that stay open while the code is here. It ends on `stateTax.ts` as the
worked example of un-shelving: same predicament last iteration, now rendering as
step 2's footnote.

### Verified

`npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run` (9 files, 581
tests, green in 10.0s — 579 before, +2 from the guard), `npm run build`. No test
of the three modules changed; they run in the same suite as before, from a new
path.

One bullet to Discovered work: the old gate bullet named four deferred items,
and this splits them three-and-one. The three shelf ones stay deferred and are
now written down where a reader of the code will find them; the 2026 state
figures bullet is live work, because `stateTax.ts` is on the page.

