## 2026-08-21 — Task: Price the third stacking effect (the 3.8% NIIT, IRC 1411)

Topmost unchecked bullet, top of Backlog 4. HEAD was clean and green before;
616 tests green after, up from 592.

### The three decisions the bullet left open

**Is "Other Ordinary Income" net investment income?** No. The field's own doc
comment has always called it "pensions, IRA withdrawals, wages, interest", and
1411(c)(5) excludes a distribution from a qualified plan or IRA by name while
wages are outside 1411(c)(1) entirely. So net investment income in this app is
`ltcg` and nothing else. `netInvestmentIncomeFor` is the one place that decides
it, and its comment argues each of the four inputs in turn — including the one
that surprised me: tax-exempt interest is out on *both* counts, because 103
keeps it out of gross income and 1411(c)(1)(A)(i) reaches interest only to the
extent it is in gross income. That makes muni the single input on this page
that moves provisional income, moves Medicare's MAGI, and leaves 1411 alone.

**Its own line, or folded into the income tax?** Both, and the split is the
point. `totalTax` stays chapter 1 exactly as it was — nothing that meant
"income tax" quietly started meaning something else — and a new
`totalFederalTax` adds chapter 2A on top. *That* is what both rate curves and
step 4's sizing now plot, because a marginal rate that stops at chapter 1 is
wrong by 3.8 points precisely where this app's subject bites. The close then
names the surtax on a line of its own (Form 8960, Schedule 2, not the tax
line), and the tax line above it splits into "$46,104 of income tax and $2,280
of the surtax on the next line" when there is one.

**An `incomeAxisFeatures` entry?** Yes — `niitEnd`, and it belongs in that
interface's own terms because it genuinely is an *end*. The surtax is 3.8% of
the *lesser* of NII and MAGI-over-threshold, so it ramps in over a band exactly
as wide as the gain and is fully priced at threshold-plus-gain; past that the
curve is flat again. Null when there is no gain, so the default chart is
untouched. With a $60,000 gain and an average benefit it lands at $239,845 of
other income — less than the $260,000 MAGI figure suggests, because the
benefits the torpedo dragged in are in AGI too — and widens the axis to
$275,000.

### The third hump, drawn

`marginalRateCurve` over a single filer with a $20,000 gain:

    199,000 → 24%      200,000 → 27.8%  ...  219,000 → 27.8%   220,000 → 24%

Twenty thousand dollars of band, exactly the size of the gain, every dollar of
it ordinary income that 1411 never taxes and that costs 3.8 cents anyway. Same
shape as the torpedo one step up, same cause: an income definition wider than
the income being taxed.

### One bug I put in and took out again

`niitFor.tax` was `toCents(NIIT_RATE * base)` at first, matching what
`IrmaaAssessment` does with premiums. That drew the band at a flat **28%**
rather than 27.8: both curves read a marginal rate off a one-dollar difference
in the total, so a half-cent of rounding is half a percentage point on the
chart. The surtax is now returned unrounded, like `totalTax`, with a comment
saying why. Anything that displays it rounds at the edge — `hereSurtax` in
App.tsx does.

`hereIncomeTax` is taken by subtraction (`hereTax - hereSurtax`) rather than by
a second `totalTax` call, so the two figures the close prints always add to the
one above them; each is then within a dollar of exact, which is the tolerance
every other whole-dollar figure on the page is quoted to.

### Verified

- 24 new tests across `tax.test.ts` (thresholds by status, unindexed across
  both years, what counts as NII, 1411's MAGI vs Medicare's on one return with
  muni, the lesser-of rule in three positions, the distribution that costs 3.8%
  anyway, `totalFederalTax` = chapter 1 + chapter 2A, both curves, the axis
  feature, conversion sizing) and `App.test.tsx` (the close's new line in all
  three of its states, the split tax line, step 3's readout, the 27.8% on "the
  next dollar", the explainer).
- `npx tsc -b`, `npx eslint src --max-warnings 0`, `npx vitest run` (9 files,
  616 tests), `npm run build`.
- Not one of the 592 pre-existing tests changed behaviour: the only edits were
  four `figure('Federal income tax')` → `figure('Federal tax')` for the
  relabelled line, and two stale counts in test names/comments.

### Left alone deliberately

The close's "no state" gloss. Adding the state name there is the *next*
unchecked bullet's whole job, and my first draft of it duplicated the step 2
footnote's exact phrase and broke that footnote's test — which is a fair
warning that it is a prose decision, not a clause to smuggle in here. Reverted.

### Filed under Discovered Work

Step 4 still has no ceiling for the 1411 threshold, and cannot reuse the
existing `'magi'` measure because that one is Medicare's (muni added back)
rather than 1411(d)'s plain AGI — it needs a seventh `ConversionMeasure`. And
the three shelved modules still call `totalTax` at nine sites, so they score
lifetime tax at chapter 1; not visible, but an inconsistency to settle when
